WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
Ultima Exodus.

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.380
This is the ultimate role-playing fantasy game.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:28.240
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.940
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.340
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.580
The day has come.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.620
We have been talking about it for a long time on this show.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.020
An RPG, a real one if you will, is finally here.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.120
It's a WRPG, not a JRPG, but I think we can all feel satisfied, right?

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:48.760
That's interesting.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.600
I've never heard that term before.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.440
Just Western RPG?

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.120
Yeah, Western RPGs.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.360
I think there's even debate if this is a CRPG computer.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.080
I'll call it, we'll call it a console port of a CRPG.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:03.340
I like that.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:09.560
Okay. I think of like WRPGs, the first thing that comes to my head is like Fallout, you know?

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.960
That like, you would not, it's definitely an RPG, but you wouldn't experience anything Final Fantasy-like in Fallout.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.780
True. Yeah. I guess the terms are interchangeable.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.860
I've always heard it just Western RPG.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.080
I've never actually heard it abbreviated like that, but I guess it makes sense if we have JRPGs.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.380
Yeah. But yeah, it's exciting to have something that's not just like a game that has some RPG elements now.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.920
Like this is an RPG, like make no mistake.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:42.220
And so we're really, you know, entering new territory here on how to do a show where a game is massive.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:49.700
You know, I think so many times the games that we review can boil, be boiled down into like what are today's mini games.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:55.060
But this is a full, like 20 hour plus game with so many nuances.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.260
And as Sean mentioned, is really like a computer game that then got like ported to the NES.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.180
So how we deal with this is just going to be a learning experience for everybody.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.940
And just bear with us through this.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:14.680
So this way, when we do some better RPGs that like people know about, like Dragon Quest and Final Fantasy, we'll, we'll find our footing.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.760
So the ultimate exit is I'm officially declaring this a test episode.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.760
What do you guys think about that?

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:34.060
I agree. I mean, when we can spend like, I don't know if it was like 30, 40 or 50 minutes on Popeye, which is basically like doing two things for maybe 15 minutes before you get sick of it.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:42.240
And then this in the same, in the same show, I hope that we don't do it for five hours, but let's just see where we take it.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.600
Now, you know, the length of the game is definitely something worth talking about.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.880
And technically, right?

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.080
Like the great arcade games of the NES, you could invest more than 20 hours into those games.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.680
I'm not sure how many people are doing that today.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:03:00.520
But I'm sure there are kids who played 20 plus hours of Donkey Kong if they loved Donkey Kong.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:08.580
Sure. But, but that kind of thing, I think what the difference is, is that kind of thing is like, there's the gameplay loop is, is more like condensed.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.880
It's like, you're doing the one thing, you're trying to get better at that one skill.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:18.720
This is like, I mean, it still has a gameplay loop, but it's just like a sprawling adventure.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:30.420
You know, you're, you're, it's closer to the Legend of Zelda in that sense, I think, where it's like, you're exploring and you're trying to get, you know, get new weapons and armor.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.620
And in this case, level up and whatnot.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.820
It's constantly new information being thrown at you.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.340
You can't just continue to do the same thing over and over again.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:48.960
It's not so much a test of skill as it is a test of preparation and perseverance through grind.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.720
And yeah, it's a completely different experience.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:04:07.820
And so given the extreme length of this NES game, you know, nowhere near as long as games today can be, but given that this is a long NES game, I, my first concern with just the game in general happens right on the very back of the box.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:13.260
You know, you're reading about this great story and you're going on an adventure and look at all these cool features.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:21.100
And one of those cool features is that, and it's advertised on the back of the box, it advertises a five-year lithium battery.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.820
Five years isn't really going to do you much good 30 years later.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.500
So what do you think here?

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.460
What do you think? Like, anybody got a working copy with the original lithium battery still?

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.640
Or does everybody's saves from 1989 are like gone now?

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:38.860
Oh, they're gone.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:40.640
Like, uh...

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:48.920
Yeah. But I also think that if I was buying this game as, like, a teenager in 89, I would have thought that five years was plenty.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.280
You know, you don't really think too far ahead when you're that young.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.320
So... Yeah, and also, like, I feel like the, just developers and stuff weren't thinking about, like,

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:04.680
weren't thinking about, like, oh, what's the legacy of this game going to be in 2021?

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.260
You know, or anything like that.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:16.300
Whereas, like, now, I feel like we're more accustomed to, like, nostalgia and, like, people look for their old, you know, Gen 2 Pokemon games and realize that they don't work anymore.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.420
And I feel like now a developer might think about something like that.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:24.260
But, like, back then, it was just like, okay, we'll release this, we'll release Ultima 4 next year, and, you know, we'll never think about this again.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.960
Well, even your own saves, though, right, gentlemen?

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:35.140
Like, how often do you get very precious about your own save files only to realize that you never have checked in on them again?

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.940
Oh, yeah, PS1 era, maybe even PS2 era.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:51.380
I've definitely got some, like, Final Fantasy 7 and 8 saves that, like, I definitely have a save in Final Fantasy 7 where I got, like, this really weird situation to occur.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.120
But, like, you know, you have to move on from that.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.760
So, yes, I just made a copy for it.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.460
You know? Yeah, I remember...

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:58.260
I haven't looked at it.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.480
Funnily enough, in Final Fantasy 8, I used to love that game.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:10.700
And any time I would get to, like, a cool cutscene or an interesting part that I really liked, I would save it in a different save slot after that.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.000
So I'd always have all of those.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.640
Like, if I ever want to go back and play this part, I can go do that.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:15.640
It's one of the greatest hits. Never did.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:20.660
Never did that. But I always had it in case I wanted to go back and play, like, an earlier part in my playthrough.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:28.100
I did something like that with Kingdom Hearts, the original Kingdom Hearts, because it gives you 99 save files.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:39.080
I would save before every boss with the idea that, like, oh, now when I'm done with this game, I can have, like, a boss rush where I can just select the save file I want and battle those bosses.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.400
And I will tell you, from experience, I never did that.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:46.140
I never went back and loaded up any of those saves and fought any of those bosses again.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.100
Maybe, like, one or two.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.520
But for the most part, that's how my save systems work.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:07:02.640
And I think that's important to start this discussion about Ultima with, because ultimately, the games that you play, though, like, they only matter while you're playing them.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:12.920
And the memories that you make during them, not these, like, 100% completions where you, like, strictly follow a guide and do everything and then have this, like, perfect save file.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.960
Well, because at the end of the day, like, you don't track your save files across your lifetime.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:23.880
Like, I cannot tell you what's going on with my, you know, earliest video games.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.260
I don't know what the save files look like for those games.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.020
And I don't care because I have those memories instead.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:36.980
So when you're going on a big journey like Ultima Exodus, the five-year lithium battery probably doesn't even matter that much in the grand scheme of things.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.220
But it also just doesn't matter at all after you're done with the game.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:49.060
Like, the important part is playing through the game and doing what you do with it, not doing it for the sake of completion.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:08:04.160
I mostly agree, but I will say that there are some games when it is, like, when I've completed a game or when I've maxed out a level, which I've only done in, you know, maybe, like, Kingdom Hearts or, like, if I've gotten a really high level in any other game or something.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:12.940
I do like to once in a while, like, go jump into, like, my level 99 file of Kingdom Hearts because I don't want to get, I don't want to grind to level 99 again.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:17.380
But it's fun to kind of explore things as a level 99 character again.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:20.800
Like, I like to keep that stuff, you know, as a little trophy.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.520
I don't know. I could also see it.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:37.880
I remember I kept a file in Red Dead before you get kind of railroaded for the rest of the game just so that, like, you know, you have that file to go and dick around and do cowboy shit if you ever want to return to it.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:42.280
So going back to that idea, I think I have actually come back.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:52.100
Yeah. So am I wrong to think, like, this is 1989, you know, it's the first RPG truly on the system.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:59.020
Dungeons and Dragons is not, you know, it's only tabletop, paper, and pen.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:02.640
And this is, like, Dungeons and Dragons coming to the NES.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:19.720
Like, this is kind of a big deal for, you know, Dungeons and Dragons fans, role-playing fans, a new way to play tabletop games because now it's, like, the game is the game master, the dungeon master who handles, like, so much of the world building for you.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:25.980
And, yeah, these things existed on Apple II and Commodore 64 for, like, a really long time before this.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:35.020
But the NES is so famous because it introduced an entire new generation to video gaming, you know?

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:41.800
Like, they weren't – not every family had an Apple II and then also went to, like, these stores and bought these games.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:44.920
So, really, like, a lot of people were only gaming on the NES.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:56.960
So this is an eye-opening moment for a lot of people just in terms of, like, what's possible in terms of storytelling and really feeling like you're going on an adventure inside of a video game.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:59.600
Something where everything's programmed for you.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:08.920
Yeah, I'm actually very surprised as somebody who didn't really know the history of RPGs on the NES.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:15.640
Like, yeah, I knew about Final Fantasy and, yeah, I had heard of Dragon Quest slash Warrior.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:33.700
But I did not think that the first RPG that we'd get on the NES isn't, like, that classic JRPG style, but is this sort of Western CRPG that isn't, like, a perfect one-to-one of D&D systems.

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:37.760
But it's much closer than any JRPG.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:45.700
And I always just thought that it would be kind of simplified because I do kind of consider JRPGs to be simplified.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:56.700
So, yeah, in 1989, people are getting, like – they've got a food system and there's, like – you're keeping track of the wind and the moon cycles.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:01.680
And, like, this is just so much information that, like, that's just what RPGs were.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:06.320
I'm kind of going off the rails here if you want to save me.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:13.980
Yeah, no, I'll save you for a second because I think you made – I think you did make one very strong point, though, of, like, it's surprising that this is the first one to come.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.160
And that's true, right?

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:22.280
Like, you would think a Japanese developer, because they did make those games in 86, 87, like, they were totally available.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:25.240
They just weren't translated and brought over to the West.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:38.420
And I think that's because the NES, by Nintendo of America's standards, was set up to be this, like, play your arcade games at home, buy this wacky dance pad, shoot ducks.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:48.860
You know, like, they weren't really selling the complications that a game like Ultima or even, you know, Final Fantasy would offer you.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:12:00.200
They were kind of, like, saying, well, we don't really know if that audience would play these kinds of games on our systems because of the limitations of an NES against an Apple II.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:16.620
You know, like, I don't think you're going to be able to sell the same people that play pen and paper to be like, oh, well, this is, like, just as good as your imagination and all the things that you can possibly control via pen and paper.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.780
Like, we're going to be able to do all that on the NES and you're going to love it.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.720
Like, maybe they weren't sold on that idea.

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:24.440
You're going to love it. You little bitch.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.860
I'm sure that was the actual conversation in the execs room.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.660
I think that was their, like, marketing campaign.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:34.700
It was, like, their commercial.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.800
And this is Ultima Exodus, not Ultima 3 Exodus.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.020
And I think that's the right move for what it's worth.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.460
I always have an opinion on titles.

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:54.960
That's very confusing to release the third Ultima game and call it Ultima 3 and pretend, like, you know, well, 1 and 2, you have to go buy other systems that we don't have.

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:59.960
Yeah. And also, like, this is your first time experiencing this and you want to go on an adventure.

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.220
Do I need to play 1 and 2 to understand 3?

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:05.260
Like, I think that was the right move to remove all that.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:08.600
But it's also not really Ultima 3 at the end of the day.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.400
This is a whole new soundtrack.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:12.960
The graphics are modified completely for the NES.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:19.940
Whereas there's considerable alterations to even, like, the gameplay in terms of how long the game is and what you can do inside of it.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:26.180
They expanded a lot of dialogue to be more clear and direct about what to do.

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:37.080
And I think, you know, in the computer version, the biggest changer probably is that in the computer version, you use a keyboard to control all your different menus and bring up different things.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.480
There is no NES keyboard peripheral.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:48.660
So they had to figure out a way to put everything that you need in menus in one particular menu that you can then shuffle through to enter other menus.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.440
I know I said menus a lot there.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.480
But the idea basically just being that...

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:53.920
Oh, yeah. This game has a lot of menus.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:59.160
Yeah. They had to figure out a way to cram all these menus into one bigger menu.

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:01.560
And it kind of works like how...

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.060
And this is the simplest explanation I can think of.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:10.020
But it works like Pokemon in that sense of, like, you hit the start button and every menu option that you'll ever need in the game pulls up.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:11.760
It's very similar to that.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:16.800
And I think that's the right call over hitting, like, F or G or H.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:18.500
You know? Like, we don't need all those keys.

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:23.960
Yeah, definitely. And I think the difference there is that, like, something like Pokemon, though, the menu is, like...

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:30.060
It's things that you want to do that are not on, like, the main field.

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:35.260
You know what I mean? Like, you want to go into your items or you want to go look at your Pokemon or look at your stats.

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:40.580
Whereas this, it's like, if you want to talk to someone, you open up a menu and you select the talk option.

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.080
Yeah, that's something...

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:43.920
And if you want to, you know, anything.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:49.620
If you want to fight, you open up the menu and you click fight and then a battle will start, you know, if you're in front of someone that you can fight.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:52.360
Yeah, that's something that I think...

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:56.920
I mean, it's definitely a sign of its age and of its time.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:02.360
But I definitely think that a lot of these things could have been context-based.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.140
Like, no one's going to want to fight a treasure chest.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:06.280
You're going to want to open it.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:10.460
Most people aren't going to try and fight a townsperson.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.480
They're probably going to want to talk to them.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:20.620
And I feel like every other, like, lesser action could have been put in those menus.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:32.240
But, yeah, it's definitely just a sign of, like, this very early realm of RPG gaming.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.180
So I get it.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:36.260
Yeah, and just the fact that it's...

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:40.160
It's not a port, you know. It's because they're trying to...

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.100
They're like, okay, we have all these commands that you do on the keyboard.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:43.500
Well, let's just put them all on a menu.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.800
And they, like, don't think more about it as to, like, well, can we go even further and make it context?

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:50.100
Can we streamline this?

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:56.240
Yeah. But although I guess now thinking of that, they probably could have streamlined it even on the keyboard version and made it like...

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:59.140
Yeah. You're talking to this person because he's a person you would talk to.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:08.400
And that's one thing I struggle with when I think about how to talk about this game is who's it really for here, you know?

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:12.420
And it's not for the usual NES audience.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:20.420
And so I kind of say, like, how much of this is, you know, programming code that they don't want to change and they don't want to work with?

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:27.120
So, yes, you can fight the treasure chest and you can choose to talk to nothing and they'll tell you no one's there, right?

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:29.280
Like, we don't need those things.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:32.620
But how much of that is programming changes that they don't want to make?

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:38.060
And how much of it is, like, the freedom to do whatever it is that you want to do, you know?

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:47.300
That, like, the fight is a command and an active choice that you make rather than running into a battle or, like, pressing the A button to talk.

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:52.660
It's like, no, you choose to talk to that person because you can also choose to fight every townsperson if you want.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.560
True. Like, I think, like, there's a decision here.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:02.600
And that's a whole other, like, conversation that we'll have later on about, you know, did they make this game for the right audience?

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:13.780
But here, and just to start things off, I'd argue that they're making this game so obtuse on purpose, as weird as that sounds.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:23.340
Like, they're giving you a lot of control and a lot of things that don't necessarily matter on purpose to show you how much this is your story and your world.

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:31.860
True. One thing I'll say just to sort of clarify my opinion, I think you should still be able to fight whoever the hell you want.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:38.880
But maybe, like, if you just press, like, the B button or the A button on the field, it'll talk.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:42.400
And then you can go into the menu and choose to fight if you want.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:44.760
Just to, like, make that a little bit easier.

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:47.880
But no, I do totally get what you're saying there.

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:53.860
That this is about your story and every action should be your own.

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:59.140
Yeah, it's like a quality of life versus, like, full immersion.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:05.620
You know, like, they've got to find that balance of, like, well, it's just easier to do this because you'll probably mostly want to just talk to townspeople.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:13.040
But it's more immersive if you know, like, I could fight these townspeople if I want and get attacked by the guards and, you know, that whole thing.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.240
